+For consequential criteria, check the actual grader against a known failure and a valid
+alternative. Compare its judgements with artifacts or traces: a required heading can appear
+while the underlying result is wrong. Investigate disagreement before trusting totals; see
+the [Agent Skills grading guidance](https://agentskills.io/skill-creation/evaluating-skills#grading-principles).
+
+## Interpreting a comparison
+
+Report distinct tasks, repeated trials and checks separately; several assertions on one
+output are not independent attempts. Retain per-case outcomes and relevant measured time
+or token costs so an aggregate does not hide a consequential regression or trade-off.
+
+If both configurations reach the ceiling, report no observed difference on those cases and
+criteria. A tie alone establishes neither equivalence nor general lack of value. Preserve valid
+safety and regression criteria even when both configurations pass; they can protect a
+contract without discriminating the comparison. If improvement remains the question, add
+cases from real coverage gaps prospectively, not by selecting only cases where the revision
+wins. See [Anthropic's evaluation guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents)
+for the task/trial distinction and capability versus regression evaluation.
+
